{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22410","model_call":10,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2706"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2706"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_88438645#1_143593631","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2706","rank":4,"snippet":"72.7% of all undergraduate and 38.7% of all graduate-level distance students. The total of 5.8 million fall 2014 distance education students is composed of 2.85 million taking all of their courses at a distance and 2.97 million taking some, but not all, distance courses Unlike a decade ago, the majority of employers are now embracing online education. As more and more trusted schools offer online degree programs, respect continues to grow. Online education is no longer an oddity but the norm as online course enrollment climbs for the 10th straight year. According to a <cover>survey conducted by CareerBuilder.com, 83 percent of executives say that “an online degree is as credible as one earned through a traditional campus-based program.” When asked, employers </cover>said that accreditation of the school, quality of its graduates, and the reputation of the college or university were among other things that make an online degree more credible.\" , \"start_char\": 421.000000 , \"title\": \"Are Online Degrees as Respected as Traditional Degrees?\" , \"url\": \"https://www.edsmart.org/are-online-degrees-respected/\" }"},"time":14},"model_calls":10,"document_judgments":8,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
