{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22410","model_call":23,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2710"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2710"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_29_1127833553#0_2542971707","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2710","rank":4,"snippet":"other resources, such as technical support and library services, before enrolling in a program. The fee structure of a program is another point of interest. Legitimate schools typically charge per credit hour, while fishy colleges charge per program. Which Professions Are Best Suited for Online Degrees? What Level of Degree Have You Earned? What Else Makes You Stand Out? The Final Word \" , \"segment\": \"Will Employers Take My Online Degree Seriously? Will Employers Take My Online Degree Seriously? March 15, 2021 | Evan Thompson Share on Social Are you ready to discover your college program? The pandemic is leading more students to ask: Will employers take my online degree <cover>seriously? We have the answer. What do employers really think about online degrees? </cover>Does it matter if they didn't come from traditional classroom settings? Will employers take them seriously? With the onset of coronavirus and traditional schools moving online, these questions are more relevant than ever.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Will Employers Take My Online Degree Seriously?\" , \"url\": \"https://thebestschools.org/magazine/employers-online-degrees/\" }"},"time":15},"model_calls":23,"document_judgments":21,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
