{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22410","model_call":30,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2712"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2712"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_491203027#8_1068132317","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2712","rank":2,"snippet":"you earn a degree from a school that lacks a campus or is perceived as being “online only.” GetEducated.com’s research shows that online-only schools are often perceived as “less rigorous” than universities that offer both campus-based and online degrees. Surveys undertaken by Get Educated over the last 20 years have found that some <cover>employers may harbor strong bias against online only colleges. 3. Your program is not regionally accredited. Of course, it is critical that your online college be accredited by an agency that is recognized by the U.S. Department of Education or the Council on Higher Education Accreditation (CHEA). Online schools that are regionally accredited, as opposed to nationally accredited, have the strongest reputation and offer the widest possibility of transfer credit and respect in the job market. To ensure the highest respect for your online degree, </cover>look for programs that come from colleges that are regionally accredited, have a residential campus, and strong local or regional reputations. Those three factors are what count when it comes to respect.\" , \"start_char\": 3742.000000 , \"title\": \"Are Online Degrees Respected? | GetEducated\" , \""},"time":14},"model_calls":30,"document_judgments":27,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
