{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22410","model_call":41,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"are online degrees respected by employers\" (+ yes accepted accreditation reputation))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"are online degrees respected by employers\" (+ yes accepted accreditation reputation))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1586499613#8_2944664126","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2717","rank":1,"snippet":"credit hour as the on-campus rate. While the tuition may be comparable, certain benefits offered by  online degree programs may help cut down on costs. For example, you may be able to save on commuting  costs or residential fees. Earning a college degree can be difficult for many individuals as they need to work full-time making it  difficult to adhere to a strict schedule. You can explore how online degrees are worth  it in-depth and how you can overcome difficulties you may face on this path. <cover>Are online degrees respected by employers? According to a study on online degrees, employers are more likely to consider the reputation </cover>of the institution from which the degree was earned as opposed to the medium of education. Online college degrees are no different from those earned in a traditional campus-based environment. In fact, many online college degree programs undergo the same stringent tests as traditional degree programs to gain accreditation. Additionally, employers who have taken online courses are more likely to prefer candidates with online degrees due to the perception that online degree programs can build soft skills in students.\" , \"start_char\": 4727.000000","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1586499613#8_2944664126","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":92},"model_calls":41,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":24,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
